The definitive source about Vlad the Impaler is the book, "Dracula: A
Biography of Vlad the Impaler, 1431-1476," by Radu Florescu and R. T.
McNally ( I think Florescu recently wrote another book on the subject).

Vlad the Impaler, 1431-76, was prince of Walachia (1456-62, and again in
1476 [Walachia was a major principality in what is now southern Romania,
which is also the location of modern Transylvania]).  He was warlike in
the extreme and fought the Turks for most of his reign.  His cruelty and
sadism were legendary throughout Europe.  War prisoners, and even those
of his subjects guilty of relatively minor crimes, were executed (by
being impaled on something, more often than not).  Vlad's victims were
displayed around his castle in their impaled state.  On occasion, Vlad
is supposed to have drunk the blood of his victims.

Vlad was deposed in 1462, reinstated in 1476, but was soon captured and
beheaded by the Turks.

Vlad's father was ``Vlad Dracul'' (Vlad the Devil), and the derivative
name for his son is ``Vlad Dracula'' (Vlad, son of the Devil).  Legends
of Vlad's cruelty persisted and grew exaggerated long after his death,
eventually culminating in the modern ``Dracula'' story.  However,
according to Florescu's evidence, there seems to be no way to exaggerate
Vlad's evil.

From the little that I've read on this subject, it seems that Nethack's
Vlad (whom I've met on a few occasions) is not nearly as nasty as his
namesake.

